---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import ContentNote from '../../components/content/ContentNote.astro';
import EvidenceTable from '../../components/content/EvidenceTable.astro';
import RelatedReading from '../../components/content/RelatedReading.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Quarterly Money Checkup: A 45-Minute Checklist | Cooked Finance"
  description="A simple quarterly financial checkup covering cash, bills, debt payments, saving, retirement progress, protection, and one next action."
  path="/guides/quarterly-money-checkup"
  eyebrow="Repeatable routine"
  heading="A 45-minute money checkup you can repeat."
  lead="Review direction, catch problems early, and leave with one decision—not twelve new chores."
  updated="August 17, 2026"
>
  <ContentNote title="Use a consistent snapshot date">
    <p>
      Account balances move every day. Choose one date near the end of each
      quarter and record every balance from that date where practical. Direction
      over several quarters is more useful than comparing mismatched days.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <p>Gather recent pay information and statements for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>checking, savings, and cash-equivalent accounts;</li>
    <li>credit cards, mortgages, and other loans;</li>
    <li>retirement and long-term investment accounts;</li>
    <li>recurring bills, insurance, and known irregular expenses; and</li>
    <li>the previous quarter’s notes, if available.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Use a private device and avoid recording full account numbers. The checklist
    needs totals and due dates, not login credentials.
  </p>

  <h2>Minutes 0–10: update the seven inputs</h2>
  <EvidenceTable caption="Quarterly Cooked Score input worksheet">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">This quarter</th><th scope="col">Previous quarter</th><th scope="col">Reason for change</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Age</th><td>_____</td><td>_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Gross annual income</th><td>$_____</td><td>$_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Essential monthly spending</th><td>$_____</td><td>$_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Liquid cash</th><td>$_____</td><td>$_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Required monthly debt payments</th><td>$_____</td><td>$_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Recurring monthly saving</th><td>$_____</td><td>$_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Vested retirement and long-term account balances</th><td>$_____</td><td>$_____</td><td>_____</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </EvidenceTable>

  <h2>Minutes 10–20: look for operational problems</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Any bill due before the income needed to pay it arrives?</li>
    <li>Any fee, rate change, subscription, or insurance renewal you did not expect?</li>
    <li>Any incorrect charge, unfamiliar transaction, or missing payment?</li>
    <li>Any annual or seasonal cost due before the next review?</li>
    <li>Any account contact information, beneficiary, or security setting that is outdated?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Deal with unauthorized activity through the financial institution’s official
    channel promptly. Do not wait for the next quarterly review.
  </p>

  <h2>Minutes 20–30: compare direction</h2>
  <p>
    Note the total Cooked Score, but spend more time on the four components.
    Write one sentence for each change. Examples: “Cash fell because the annual
    insurance bill was paid,” or “DTI improved after the car loan ended.” This
    separates expected movement from a pattern that needs action.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not compare a legacy model result with version 2.0.0. Do not interpret a
    small rounded change as proof that a strategy succeeded or failed.
  </p>

  <h2>Minutes 30–40: review what the score cannot see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do insurance coverages and deductibles still fit the household?</li>
    <li>Are investment fees, asset allocation, and risk still understood?</li>
    <li>Have dependants, beneficiaries, employment benefits, taxes, or legal documents changed?</li>
    <li>Are near-term goals being held in an appropriate place for their time horizon?</li>
    <li>Is anyone relying on the same emergency money for two different goals?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These questions may require a qualified adviser, tax professional, insurance
    professional, or attorney. Cooked Finance does not evaluate them.
  </p>

  <h2>Minutes 40–45: make one decision</h2>
  <p>Finish this sentence:</p>
  <p><strong>Before __________, I will __________ by __________ because __________.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Give the action a date, amount, or trigger. “Save more” is a wish; “move $25
    on the day after each payday” is testable. Put the next quarterly review on
    the calendar, then stop.
  </p>

  <h2>Good reasons to review sooner</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Income loss, new job, retirement, or a substantial pay change</li>
    <li>Marriage, separation, birth, death, caregiving, or a household move</li>
    <li>New debt, refinance, delinquency, or a paid-off account</li>
    <li>A major medical, housing, vehicle, or insurance event</li>
    <li>Suspected fraud or unauthorized transactions</li>
  </ul>

  <RelatedReading links={[
    { href: '/financial-health-score/', label: 'Review the seven inputs', description: 'Keep definitions, personal scope, and time periods consistent.' },
    { href: '/guides/how-to-improve-financial-health/', label: 'Choose one improvement', description: 'Turn the review into a small repeatable action.' },
    { href: '/methodology/', label: 'Understand score changes', description: 'Check weights, anchors, rounding, and version history.' },
  ]} />
</PageLayout>
